    practical: [
      {
        title: "Task 1 — Read Your System's IPL Log in SDSF",
        description: "After every IPL the system log records the entire startup sequence. Open SDSF LOG (or the operator log via SDSF SYSLOG) and scroll back to your most recent IPL — the IEAxxxI messages from NIP, the IEE messages from the Master Scheduler, the $HASPxxx from JES2, IRR from RACF, IST from VTAM, EZB/EZZ from TCP/IP, all in chronological order. This is the canonical 'how does my system actually IPL?' inquiry that every new sysprog runs on day one. No special privilege required beyond SDSF READ.",
        hints: [
          "Hint 1: Jump to SDSF with =SD then type LOG + Enter. The current system log appears with the most recent messages at the bottom.",
          "Hint 2: To find the last IPL, use the FIND command on the LOG panel: FIND 'IEA371I' (the 'NIP processing complete' message) or FIND 'IPL' or FIND 'IEE254I'. Press F5 (RFIND) to repeat the search backward.",
          "Hint 3: Useful messages to look for and what they mean: IEA371I 'NIP processing complete'; IEE389I 'BCP data sets allocation complete'; IEE0541I 'MASTER address space initialised'; $HASP423 'JES2 ... starting'; IRR812I 'RACF service ... ACTIVE'; EZZ4202I 'TCP/IP terminated' / EZZ4202I 'TCP/IP started'.",
          "Hint 4: Note timestamps on each message — calculate how long each phase took. Compare with the realistic timeline section in the content above.",
          "Hint 5: To see only IEA-prefix messages: type FILTER then a pattern; or use SDSF's ULOG / OPERLOG views for richer filtering."
        ],
        solution: "<strong>Expected SDSF LOG content from a recent IPL:</strong><pre style=\"background:#0d0d0d;color:#ffb000;padding:1rem;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:'Share Tech Mono',monospace;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.45;margin:.6rem 0;\">N 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:00:01.42 *MASTER*  IEA371I SYS0.IPLPARM ON DEVICE 0A80 SELECTED FOR IPL PARAMETERS\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:00:01.45 *MASTER*  IEA980I IEASYS00 IS THE SYSTEM PARAMETER MEMBER\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:00:01.51 *MASTER*  IEA008I LOAD ADDRESS = 0A80, LOADPARM = 0A80FF\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:00:02.78 *MASTER*  IEE252I MEMBER IEASYS00 FOUND IN SYS1.PARMLIB\n... (page-dataset opens, catalog init, console init - ~1-2 minutes of IEA / IEE messages) ...\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:01:48.04 *MASTER*  IEA371I NIP PROCESSING COMPLETE\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:01:48.23 *MASTER*  IEE534I MASTER SCHEDULER INITIALIZATION COMPLETE\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:01:50.11 *MASTER*  $HASP423 JES2 STARTING\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:01:51.55 *MASTER*  IRR812I RACF SERVICE STARTUP COMPLETE\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:02:14.30 *MASTER*  IEE0541I VTAM INITIALIZATION COMPLETE\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:02:31.78 *MASTER*  EZZ4202I TCP/IP STARTED\nN 0000000 SY1     2026123 09:03:48.92 CICSAOR1  DFHSI1517 CICSAOR1 CONTROL IS BEING GIVEN TO CICS\n... (more subsystems coming up over the next few minutes) ...</pre><strong>What this teaches:</strong> the abstract IPL phases from the content become concrete timestamps. You can measure how long each phase took on your specific system: hardware IPL → NIP → Master Scheduler ready → JES2 starts → RACF active → CICS regions ready. Notice the message prefixes — IEAxxxI for early NIP, IEExxxI for Master Scheduler, $HASPxxx for JES2, IRRxxxI for RACF, IST for VTAM, EZZ/EZB for TCP/IP, DFHxxxx for CICS, DSNxxxxI for Db2. Once you recognise these prefixes you can scan a LOG of thousands of lines and immediately know which subsystem each message is from.<br><br><strong>Variations:</strong> (a) compute the elapsed time from IEA371I 'NIP processing complete' to your most recent CICS DFHSI1517 — that's the gap between 'OS is ready' and 'applications are ready,' typically the dominant part of a real IPL; (b) use SDSF OPERLOG (richer than SYSLOG, covers the entire sysplex) if your site has it active; (c) hunt for any messages with severity 'E' or 'W' during IPL — those flag potential issues that should be investigated."
      },
      {
        title: "Task 2 — IPL a Hercules / Hyperion Mainframe Emulator (Bonus)",
        description: "If you have a desktop machine and a few hours, you can run an entire z/OS or MVS 3.8j system inside Hercules — the open-source mainframe emulator — or its modern fork Hyperion. Watching a real IPL play out from the emulator's console gives you the unfiltered view of every IEA message from the very first instruction the simulated CPU executes, which is impossible to see on a shared production system. The same exercise also teaches you the HMC equivalents (LOAD device, LOADPARM) hands-on.",
        hints: [
          "Hint 1: Hercules is at https://hercules-390.github.io and the Hyperion fork at https://github.com/SDL-Hercules-390/hyperion. Both run on Linux, Windows, and macOS. There is no licensed z/OS image included — you must obtain one separately.",
          "Hint 2: For learning, two pre-packaged systems work without IBM licensing: TK4- (a complete MVS 3.8j 'turnkey' from moshix.org) and the Marist College z/OS 1.13 ADCD (educational use only, requires an IBM master-the-mainframe-style enrolment). TK4- is the simplest entry point.",
          "Hint 3: Install Hercules, download TK4- and unpack it. The package contains a hercules.cnf configuration file, a directory of DASD volume images (\".dasd\" files), and start scripts. The IPL device for TK4- is typically address 148 (the SYSRES volume).",
          "Hint 4: Start Hercules with the TK4- config (e.g., './mvs' on Linux). The Hercules console opens. Connect a 3270 terminal emulator (x3270, c3270, or wc3270) to localhost:3270 — that becomes your operator console.",
          "Hint 5: From the Hercules console type 'ipl 148' and press Enter. Watch the operator console: you will see the bootstrap CCWs execute, IEAIPL00 load the nucleus, NIP run, and the system progress to ready in 1-3 minutes (modest emulated hardware).",
          "Hint 6: Once ready, the operator console accepts commands. Try 'D A,L' (display active address spaces), 'D U,DASD,ONLINE' (online DASD volumes), 'D T' (current time). Type 'Z EOD' followed by 'QUIESCE' to shut the system down cleanly when you're done."
        ],
        solution: "<strong>Expected operator console output during a TK4- IPL:</strong><pre style=\"background:#0d0d0d;color:#ffb000;padding:1rem;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:'Share Tech Mono',monospace;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.45;margin:.6rem 0;\">HHC00007I Previous message from function 'ipl_main' at ipl.c(289)\nHHC02263I Loading device 0148 ...\nIEA371I SYS1.PARMLIB ON VOLUME MVSRES SELECTED FOR IPL PARAMETERS\nIEA101I SYSTEM PARAMETERS USED:\n         IEASYS00\nIEA008I LOAD ADDRESS = 0148, LOADPARM = ........\nIEA371I NIP PROCESSING COMPLETE\nIEE534I MASTER SCHEDULER INITIALIZATION COMPLETE\nIEE0541I JES2 IS ACTIVE\n$HASP423 JES2 STARTING\nIEE039I COMMAND COMPLETE\nIRR812I RACF/PROD SERVICE INITIALIZATION COMPLETE\nIEE389I MVS COMMAND PROCESSING AVAILABLE\n00- IEE600I REPLY TO 00 IS;NOREPLY</pre>The Hercules console (HHC-prefixed messages) is the emulator-level trace; the IEA/IEE/IRR messages are the real z/OS IPL output, identical to what you saw in Task 1 — just running on emulated hardware on your desktop instead of a real IBM Z mainframe.<br><br><strong>What this teaches:</strong> (a) IPL is real and observable from byte zero — you literally watch CCWs read the boot record and load IEAIPL00; (b) the same message prefixes (IEA, IEE, $HASP, IRR) appear regardless of hardware; (c) operator commands like 'D A,L' and 'D U,DASD,ONLINE' work the same on any z/OS, real or emulated; (d) IPL on emulated hardware is not slower than on real big-iron — TK4- IPLs in 1-3 minutes from cold; the timing in real production is dominated by application subsystem warmup, not IPL itself.<br><br><strong>Caveats:</strong> running real z/OS (vs MVS 3.8j) on Hercules requires an IBM-licensed image; TK4-'s MVS 3.8j is freely redistributable because IBM released it to the public domain in 1981, but it's a 1980s-era system without modern features (no zFS, no USS, no TCP/IP unless added separately). Hyperion adds modern emulator features (multi-CPU, faster instruction interpretation) but does not change the licensing situation. For real z/OS the legitimate options are zPDT (IBM's own emulator, sold via partner channels) or paid cloud-mainframe access (Wazi, IBM Cloud Z and Cloud Mainframe)."
      }
    ]
