        solution: "<strong>Expected SDSF DA panel (sample with PREFIX MQ*):</strong><pre style=\"background:#0d0d0d;color:#ffb000;padding:1rem;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:'Share Tech Mono',monospace;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.45;margin:.6rem 0;\">JOBNAME    OWNER    CLASS  ASID    CPU%   REAL   SIO   PAGING\nMQA1MSTR   STCMQM   STC    00E0    0.04   24K     2     0\nMQA1CHIN   STCMQM   STC    00E1    0.06   18K     5     0\nMQA1AMSM   STCMQM   STC    00E2    0.01    8K     0     0\nMQA2MSTR   STCMQM   STC    00F0    0.02   22K     1     0\nMQA2CHIN   STCMQM   STC    00F1    0.03   16K     2     0\n   /* note: MQA2 has no AMSM — message-level security not enabled */\n</pre><strong>Drilling into MQA1MSTR's JESMSGLG (FIND 'CSQY'):</strong><pre style=\"background:#0d0d0d;color:#ffb000;padding:.6rem;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:'Share Tech Mono',monospace;font-size:.85rem;margin:.4rem 0;\">CSQY000I !MQA1 IBM MQ for z/OS  V9.4.0\nCSQY100I !MQA1 ARC RECOVERY LOG INITIALIZATION COMPLETED\nCSQY220I !MQA1 ACTIVE LOG DSN=MQM.MQA1.LOGCOPY1.DS01 OPENED\nCSQI004I !MQA1 RESOLUTION OF INDOUBT THREADS COMPLETED\nCSQR201I !MQA1 ELAPSED TIME 12.3 CPU TIME 4.1\nCSQ9022I !MQA1 IBM MQ for z/OS V9.4.0 START COMMAND ACCEPTED\nCSQY022I !MQA1 IBM MQ for z/OS V9.4.0 STARTED</pre>The CSQY022I 'STARTED' line is the canonical 'queue manager fully up' marker. Note the !MQA1 prefix — that is the queue manager's <strong>command prefix (CPF)</strong>, the 1–8 character string operators use to direct console commands to this specific QMgr (e.g., <code>!MQA1 DISPLAY QMGR ALL</code> at the operator console).<br><br><strong>What you've learned:</strong> like Db2, MQ isn't one process; it's a coordinated group of address spaces. The xxxxMSTR/xxxxCHIN naming pattern lets you immediately identify each queue manager and its networking status. Multiple queue managers on one z/OS image is normal — production-vs-test isolation, version coexistence during upgrades, queue-sharing groups, regional vs cross-site instances. The CSQ-prefixed messages are MQ's equivalent of Db2's DSN messages and CICS's DFH messages — once you recognise the prefix you know which subsystem any log line is from."
