        solution: "<strong>From the MVS console (sample CPF !MQA1):</strong><pre style=\"background:#0d0d0d;color:#ffb000;padding:1rem;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:'Share Tech Mono',monospace;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.45;margin:.6rem 0;\">!MQA1 DISPLAY QMGR ALL\n\n  CSQM201I !MQA1 CSQMDRTC DISPLAY QMGR DETAILS\n  QMNAME(MQA1)\n  COMMANDQ(SYSTEM.COMMAND.INPUT)  CHANNEL INITIATOR(STARTED)\n  DEADQ(SYSTEM.DEAD.LETTER.QUEUE) DEFXMITQ(SYSTEM.DEFAULT.XMIT.QUEUE)\n  CCSID(00037)                    PSID(SYSTEM.SHARED)\n  ...\n  CSQ9022I !MQA1 CSQMDRTC ' DISPLAY QMGR' NORMAL COMPLETION</pre><pre style=\"background:#0d0d0d;color:#ffb000;padding:1rem;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:'Share Tech Mono',monospace;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.45;margin:.6rem 0;\">!MQA1 DISPLAY QLOCAL(*) CURDEPTH IPPROCS OPPROCS\n\n  CSQM201I !MQA1\n  QUEUE(SYSTEM.ADMIN.COMMAND.QUEUE)   CURDEPTH(0)   IPPROCS(1)   OPPROCS(0)\n  QUEUE(SYSTEM.DEAD.LETTER.QUEUE)     CURDEPTH(2)   IPPROCS(0)   OPPROCS(0)\n  QUEUE(SYSTEM.DEFAULT.LOCAL.QUEUE)   CURDEPTH(0)   IPPROCS(0)   OPPROCS(0)\n  QUEUE(ORDER.IN)                     CURDEPTH(47)  IPPROCS(2)   OPPROCS(8)\n  QUEUE(ORDER.OUT)                    CURDEPTH(0)   IPPROCS(0)   OPPROCS(0)\n  QUEUE(PAYMENT.IN)                   CURDEPTH(112) IPPROCS(0)   OPPROCS(3)\n  ...\n  CSQ9022I !MQA1 ' DISPLAY QLOCAL' NORMAL COMPLETION</pre><strong>What this output reveals:</strong> ORDER.IN has 47 messages waiting and 2 apps actively GETting (IPPROCS=2) plus 8 producers connected (OPPROCS=8) — healthy traffic. PAYMENT.IN has 112 messages but no consumers (IPPROCS=0) — that's a backlog symptom; either the consumer crashed or wasn't started. SYSTEM.DEAD.LETTER.QUEUE has 2 messages — investigate why MQ couldn't deliver them.<pre style=\"background:#0d0d0d;color:#ffb000;padding:1rem;border-radius:4px;overflow-x:auto;font-family:'Share Tech Mono',monospace;font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.45;margin:.6rem 0;\">!MQA1 DISPLAY CHSTATUS(*)\n\n  CHANNEL(MQA2.SDR)        CHLTYPE(SDR)    STATUS(RUNNING)\n  CHANNEL(MQA2.RCVR)       CHLTYPE(RCVR)   STATUS(RUNNING)\n  CHANNEL(LIVE.SVRCONN)    CHLTYPE(SVRCONN) STATUS(RUNNING)\n  CHANNEL(MQ3.SDR)         CHLTYPE(SDR)    STATUS(RETRYING)\n  ...</pre>MQ3.SDR is RETRYING — the remote QMgr MQ3 is currently unreachable. Drill into it with <code>!MQA1 DISPLAY CHSTATUS(MQ3.SDR) ALL</code> to see the last error code (LSTMSGI), how many retries happened, and the next-retry interval.<br><br><strong>What you've learned:</strong> MQSC is the daily admin language. The same DISPLAY QLOCAL / DISPLAY CHSTATUS commands work identically on Linux/Windows MQ — once you've memorised them they transfer everywhere. Patterns to internalise: queue depth + IPPROCS = does this backlog have consumers; channel STATUS = is my MQ network working; DLQ depth > 0 = something undelivered, investigate. The full DISPLAY-everything cheat-sheet (DISPLAY QMGR / QLOCAL / QREMOTE / QALIAS / CHANNEL / CHSTATUS / LSSTATUS / TOPIC / SUB / CONN / THREAD) covers about 90% of operational MQ inquiries. For destructive actions (DEFINE, ALTER, DELETE, START, STOP) you'll need MQ admin authority, which most learners don't have — those are read in this card and exercised on a sandbox QMgr if you set up your own."
